WEBVTT

1.1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.229
For hundreds of years, the best clocks in the world were just a weight swinging back and forth.

1.2
00:00:05.229 --> 00:00:08.256
Why does a swinging weight make such a good timekeeper?

2.1
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:18.059
The story goes that around fifteen eighty-three, a young Galileo watched a lamp swing in a cathedral, and timed it with his pulse.

2.2
00:00:18.059 --> 00:00:21.753
Big swings and small swings seemed to take the same time.

3.1
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.784
The time for one full swing is called the period.

3.2
00:00:25.784 --> 00:00:34.277
For small swings it depends only on the length L and gravity g: T equals two pi times the square root of L over g.

4.1
00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:38.228
Notice what is missing: the size of the swing.

4.2
00:00:38.228 --> 00:00:42.820
Start it at ten degrees or twenty, and the period is almost the same.

4.3
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.879
That is why it keeps steady time even as it slowly dies down.

5.1
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:51.863
What does matter is length. Quadruple the length and the period doubles.

5.2
00:00:51.863 --> 00:00:56.267
So a clockmaker can tune a clock simply by moving the bob up or down.

6.1
00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:03.621
But a real pendulum loses energy to air and friction, and each swing gets smaller.

6.2
00:01:03.621 --> 00:01:06.307
Left alone, it would eventually stop.

7.1
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.439
The fix is the escapement.

7.2
00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:19.268
A falling weight or a spring pushes the gears, and the escapement lets them advance one tooth per swing, giving the pendulum a tiny push each time.

8.1
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:24.784
Each tick you hear is a gear tooth being caught and released.

8.2
00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:29.172
The pendulum sets the rhythm, and the weight supplies the energy.

8.3
00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:31.467
The gears simply count the swings.

9.1
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:36.475
So a pendulum keeps time because its swing depends on length, not on size.

9.2
00:01:36.475 --> 00:01:41.083
Add a little push to beat friction, and a swinging weight becomes a clock.
